  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b5bd5e60e42c9624b876a36ed587e020, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  infoTodisplay: The Burmese Python (Python bivittatus) is an invasive species of
    predator which has quickly taken over the Everglades. Because of their large
    size, adult Burmese pythons have few predators, with humans being the exception.
    They prey upon native species and may reduce their populations locally. Severe
    declines in mammal populations throughout the Everglades have been linked to
    Burmese pythons, with the most severe declines in native species having occurred
    in the remote southernmost regions of the Everglades where pythons have been
    established the longest. As one of the largest snakes in the world, they can
    grow to impressive sizes. Adult Burmese pythons caught in Florida average between
    6 and 9 feet. Generally their diet consists of various mammals, birds and reptiles.
  textBoxToReveal: {fileID: 2847044717783916734}
  canvasBase: {fileID: 6826241143585982359}
  interactAudio: {fileID: 3601877167380916910}
  canvasPopupHeight: 5
